INT. ROLLER RINK - AFTER HOURS

Dim. A mirror ball turns slowly, throwing pale spots across an empty floor. A jukebox hums at low volume.

CLEO MARCHAND, 20s, in a staff polo and skates, mops the rink under a single working light. BRAM NAKAMURA-COLE, 30s, the night manager, counts the register at the snack counter. DOT WINTERBOURNE, 60s, the cleaner, sweeps popcorn from the carpet by the lockers.

CLEO
(to Bram)
You hear that?

BRAM
Hear what?

CLEO
Skates. Out on the floor.

BRAM
That's the mop.

CLEO
The mop doesn't have wheels.

Bram looks up. Dot stops sweeping. The mirror ball keeps turning.

DOT
(to the room)
Anybody else still in here, we're closed.

No answer. The jukebox clicks. A song begins that nobody chose, a slow waltz.

BRAM
Dot, did you play that?

DOT
Not me.

CLEO
(muttering)
Okay. Okay, okay.

She backs toward the counter, mop held in front of her like a spear. Out on the floor, in the circling light, a single set of wheels rolls by with no one on it. Around the rink. Once. Twice.

BRAM
Nobody panic.

CLEO
I'm not panicking.

Dot drops the broom. The skates stop in the middle of the floor. They turn, slowly, to face the three of them.

DOT
Go. Go!

The three scatter. Cleo vaults the counter. Bram drops the cash drawer, coins ringing across the tile. Dot sprints for the lockers, slams the door, and holds it shut. The skates roll toward the counter. The waltz swells.

CLEO
(whispering to herself)
Please be a prank. Please be a prank.
